# Deployment terminology (Don / Stock-Pattern-Stream)

Use this vocabulary in planning, git, and deploy discussions so **LOCAL** and **LIVE** are never conflated.

## LOCAL environment (synonyms — all mean the same thing)

- **LOCAL**
- **DEV** (meaning: development on the machine, *not* a cloud “dev server” unless one exists and is named explicitly)
- **My Machine**
- **My PC**

**Definition:** The working copy and runtime **on Don’s computer**: editor, checked-out branch, uncommitted changes, `npm run dev`, local DB if applicable. Nothing here is “live” for end users until it is built, pushed, and deployed.

When Don says “fix it on DEV” or “on My Machine”, interpret as **local workspace only**, not the Render URL.

## LIVE environment (synonyms — all mean the same thing)

- **Live**
- **Production**
- **Live App**
- **Main** (meaning: the **primary shipped product** / hosted app users hit — *not* “any git branch named main” in isolation)

**Definition:** The **deployed** application (e.g. Render web service after a successful deploy). Usually tracks the default integration branch on GitHub (often git `main`), but **“Main” in conversation = hosted Live App**, not “whatever branch is checked out locally.”

When Don says “push live” or “get this to Production”, interpret as **promote LOCAL changes through git to the branch the host deploys, then deploy** — not “assume `origin/main` already matches my laptop without verifying.”

## Disambiguation

- **Git branch `main`:** A branch name. Often what Live deploys from; still not “Live” until that commit is what the host is running.
- **Avoid** using the single word **“dev”** without **LOCAL** or **My Machine** if it could be read as “a server” or “non-production cloud.” Prefer **LOCAL** or **DEV (My Machine)**.
